<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        A SENTENCE WITH A WAY TO ACT ON IT UNDER IT - a failure with "Force stop..." and, since
        2026-09-24, a warning with "Also stop it". Styles and templates only, not one value.

        ITS OWN FILE SINCE 2026-09-24, AND THE MARKUP RATCHET IS WHAT ASKED, for the third time in
        this corner of the window. PlanLines.xaml took the offer under a warning and went past the
        line where a markup file counts as crowding its ceiling. The seam was already there: these
        two are the only lines of a plan that carry a button, and a button under a sentence is one
        subject - the press bubbles to the sheet, which decides what it means. Everything left in
        PlanLines.xaml is a line somebody reads, and everything here is a line somebody can act on.

        MERGED AFTER PlanLines.xaml AND BEFORE Plan.xaml, AND NEITHER IS OPTIONAL. The quiet offer
        extends PlanCopyButton, which PlanLines.xaml declares, and the loud one extends ElevateAction
        from Controls.xaml - and StaticResource resolves WHILE A FILE IS BEING READ, so a dictionary
        merged after this one is a name that does not exist yet. App.xaml carries the order, and
        three places repeat it: WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe, and the pool in
        AppearanceGuards.

        NOTHING HERE BINDS A Click. A resource dictionary has no code-behind, so each button carries
        its item in Tag and PlanView catches the press as it bubbles.
    -->
    <!--
        A FAILURE, AND UNDER THE ONES THAT CAN BE ESCALATED, THE WAY OUT OF IT. Added 2026-09-07,
        and the shape is the sentence template above with one thing that appears under a few of
        them.

        THE OFFER STANDS UNDER ITS OWN SENTENCE RATHER THAN IN THE FOOTER, which is `docs/11` 9.2
        point 4: nothing is asked about a thing whose effect was not shown. A second button in the
        footer would be detached from the entry it is about - a plan over twenty rows can fail on
        any of them - and it would put two destructive-looking asks side by side in the one part of
        the sheet that is always on the screen.

        IT WEARS ElevateAction, WHICH IS ALREADY THE STYLE FOR "HERE IS THE WAY OUT OF THAT RED
        SENTENCE" - the status line's elevate button is the other one. An outline rather than a
        fill, because the fill is spoken for: on this window a red FILL means "pressing this changes
        your machine", and this press changes nothing at all. It opens a plan.

        THE BRUSH IS MeaningRejected AND NOT MeaningUntrusted, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION TO THE
        DESIGN RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. That design asked for #F1707A on the strength of its 5.69
        ratio - which is measured against the WINDOW. This sheet is a panel, and everything on it
        stands on SurfacePanel. Measured with tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 -Background '#343434' on
        2026-09-07: #F1707A reads 4,35 there, under the 4,5 WCAG SC 1.4.3 asks, and #F47F88 reads
        4,88. It is the same measurement that moved the rejected role off that colour on 2026-09-02,
        backlog 286, and the design was reading the figure from before that move. ContrastGuards
        would not have caught it, because it checks every brush against the window.

        THE WORD CARRIES IT AND NOT THE COLOUR - WCAG 1.4.1. It says "Force stop" and it is a
        button, so a screen with no colour at all still says what it is.

        THE FAILURE TRAVELS ON THE BUTTON IN Tag, and there is no Click here, both for the reasons
        written out at PlanCommandTemplate in PlanLines.xaml.
    -->
    <!--
        IT GOES AWAY BY TRIGGER RATHER THAN BY CONVERTER, AND THAT IS THE RULE THIS FILE LIVES
        UNDER rather than a preference. A theme file may hold a Style or a DataTemplate and nothing
        else - AppearanceGuards holds that whitelist - so the BooleanToVisibilityConverter the
        sheet's own markup uses six times cannot be declared here, and a StaticResource reaching for
        one declared over in PlanView.xaml resolves against nothing at parse time. A DataTrigger
        says the same thing with the tools this file is allowed.

        COLLAPSED IS THE DEFAULT AND VISIBLE IS THE TRIGGER, never the other way round. Most
        failures offer nothing, so the state that has to be right when a binding fails is the one
        where no button appears.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanForceButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource ElevateAction}">
        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding HasOffer}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanFailureTemplate">
        <StackPanel Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Text}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Foreground="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />

            <Button Style="{StaticResource PlanForceButton}"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planForce"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Label}"
                    Content="{Binding Label}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.offer.hint}"
                    Tag="{Binding}" />
        </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        A SENTENCE UNDER "WORTH KNOWING", AND UNDER THE ONE SAYING A DISABLED ENTRY KEEPS RUNNING, THE
        OFFER TO STOP IT IN THE SAME PLAN. 2026-09-24, UX-GUI-006 and spec C4 - the owner chose to have
        the press rebuild this sheet with the stop as a second step (PlanWarningLine says why one
        offer per sheet, and why none on a record).

        THE SHAPE IS PlanFailureTemplate'S, BECAUSE THE SITUATION IS: a sentence, and under a few of
        them a way to act on it, collapsed by default so a binding that fails shows no button.

        THE RED OUTLINE OF "Force stop...", THE OWNER'S CHOICE OF 2026-09-24 made on photographs of
        both. The quiet outline of "Copy" was built first, on the argument that red on this sheet
        means the way out of a red sentence and this sentence is not red. The owner weighed that and
        chose the red, so the offer is the failure's button under another name - based on it rather
        than copied from it (GUI rule 7), and kept as a name of its own so the two can part again
        without touching the template.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanOfferButton" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanForceButton}" />

    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanWarningTemplate">
        <StackPanel Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Text}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Foreground="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />

            <Button Style="{StaticResource PlanOfferButton}"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planAlsoStop"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Label}"
                    Content="{Binding Label}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.offer.alsoStop.hint}"
                    Tag="{Binding}" />
        </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>
</ResourceDictionary>
